\section{Space and Neighborhood Replace a Single Control Thread}

A cellular automaton updates a lattice of cells from local neighborhoods. Conway's Life uses a binary two-dimensional grid and the rule B3/S23: a dead cell is born with three live neighbors; a live cell survives with two or three \cite{toffoli1987cellular}. This actual Life RLE fragment encodes a glider; \texttt{b}, \texttt{o}, \texttt{\$}, and \texttt{!} denote dead runs, live runs, new rows, and the end:

\begin{verbatim}
x = 3, y = 3, rule = B3/S23
bo$2bo$3o!
\end{verbatim}

The conventional Life interpretation adds a Moore neighborhood, an infinite dead background, and synchronous generations. Those are semantic assumptions, not consequences of every spatial file format. Synchronous update reads generation \(t\) and writes generation \(t+1\). Updating cells in place in traversal order yields a different system. Hexagonal neighborhoods, finite wrapping domains, stochastic transitions, mobile agents, and spatial fields need different coordinates and state models.

\section{Represent Neighborhoods and Time Explicitly}

A spatial IR records domain dimensionality and extents, coordinate system, boundary condition, neighborhood offsets, cell state type, transition region, random source, and update discipline. A double-buffered implementation is one lowering, not the semantics. Sparse frontiers and tiles can implement the same generation relation if absent cells and boundaries are handled consistently.

