Generated artifacts can include CPU or GPU kernels, halo-exchange schedules, partition maps, boundary kernels, initial-state loaders, and frame or checkpoint streams. A visualization is an output consumer; color mapping and frame skipping must not be mistaken for automaton state transitions.

% Annotated walkthrough
\section{Tile a Synchronous Generation Safely}

\begin{verbatim}
old := current_generation
partition domain into disjoint output tiles
parallel for tile:
    halo := gather old cells required by neighborhood and boundary rule
    for coordinate in tile:
        next[coordinate] := transition(old[coordinate], halo_neighbors)
barrier until every tile has finished
swap(old, next)
\end{verbatim}

Tiling, vectorization, bit packing, sparse-frontier evaluation, and parallel execution are legal when every output cell reads the same logical old generation. Skipping a cell requires proof that its state and all affected neighborhoods remain unchanged. Stencil fusion across generations expands the required halo and must preserve intermediate boundary behavior. Random rules need counter-based or otherwise reproducible streams indexed by logical coordinate and generation if schedule independence is promised.

Validation compares generation hashes and selected cell traces against a simple reference over finite windows, including edges, corners, empty worlds, oscillators, and patterns crossing tile boundaries. Static checks confirm that the kernel writes only its output tile and that halo widths cover every neighborhood offset. For infinite sparse domains, compare the finite live set under a declared background state. Preservation concerns logical generations and spatial coordinates, not thread order, tile shape, or rendering cadence.

\section{Test a Halo With an Oscillator Across a Tile Boundary}

In Life, put live cells at \((0,1),(1,1),(2,1)\) on a sufficiently large board away from its boundary, or on an infinite plane. Split the tiles between columns zero and one. The next generation has live cells at \((1,0),(1,1),(1,2)\); one more step restores the horizontal line. Birth at \((1,0)\) needs one cell from the neighboring tile, so a compiler that omits the one-cell halo will miss it.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lll}
\hline
Cell & Old neighbor count & New status \\
\hline
\((0,1)\) & 1 & dies \\
\((1,1)\) & 2 & survives \\
\((1,0)\) & 3 & born \\
\((2,0)\) & 2 & remains dead \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\begin{verbatim}
for tile in tiles in parallel:
    for position in tile.interior:
        count := 0
        for offset in moore_neighbors_excluding_center:
            count += read_old_with_boundary(position + offset)
        if read_old(position):
            next[position] := count in {2, 3}
        else:
            next[position] := count == 3
barrier()
swap(old, next)
\end{verbatim}

For one generation, a radius-one stencil needs a one-cell halo including corners. To execute two generations per exchange without communicating intermediate values, load enough of the previous generation for a two-cell halo and compute the intermediate results in the halo as well as the tile. A one-cell halo alone cannot supply every dependency at depth two. Test the oscillator with a reversed tile traversal, or translate it so a birth depends on a diagonal neighbor in another tile. Keep a separate edge fixture for a finite toroidal domain: the expected neighbor there wraps, whereas a fixed-dead boundary provides zero.

\paragraph{Check the background before choosing a sparse representation.}
For Life an all-dead neighborhood stays dead. A rule where the same
neighborhood becomes alive cannot use ``omitted means dead'' in every
generation on an infinite plane. One alternative tracks a uniform
background for each instant and represents deviations from it. Test
an empty initial world under both rules; the next background differs.
The decision to store a tile, a set of live cells, or a set of
deviations depends on the transition as well as on the initial data.
For a changing background, derive the next background before deciding
which computed cells differ from it.
